rgin": -15.22, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.83, "baseline_margin": -11.45, "analyst_adjustment": -2.55, "key_factors": ["Strong Republican district with a -10 point partisan lean", "Incumbent Bob Onder running with a large fundraising edge ($950K vs $43K)", "All nine expert ratings call the race Safe or Solid R", "Prediction markets price Democratic chances at about 4%"], "rationale": "The district's -10 Republican lean, Onder's incumbency and overwhelming financial advantage, and a unanimous Safe/Solid R consensus among raters all point the same direction, with markets giving Mann roughly a 4% chance. The redrawn map survived legal challenges, removing the one plausible source of uncertainty.", "watch": "A late redistricting ruling forcing a materially different map, or a surge of Democratic outside spending, could narrow the expected margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Bob Onder (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Bob Onder seeks a full term in Missouri's 3rd District after winning the August primary, facing Democrat Bethany Mann in a rematch. The district leans Republican by about ten points, and Onder holds a decisive financial edge, having raised roughly $950,000 to Mann's $43,000 with little cash remaining on the Democratic side. A Libertarian candidate also appears on the ballot.\n\nEvery major race rater places this contest in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and prediction markets give Mann only about a 4% chance of winning. Litigation over Missouri's new congressional map reached the Supreme Court, but the redrawn lines remain in place for this election. With no public polling available, the race rests on fundamentals, which all favor Onder by a wide margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0408, "margin": -11.57}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0449, "margin": -12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.04